# Goldrock Financial Web Style & Design Rules

## 1. Brand & Color Theme
- **Primary Color:** Use a rich gold/yellow (#FFD700 or similar, matching your logo) for accents, icons, buttons, and highlights.
- **Secondary Color:** Use deep black or charcoal (#222 or #000) for headers, navigation, and text.
- **Background:** Use white (#FFF) or very light gray (#F9F9F9) for backgrounds to keep the site clean and modern.
- **Accent Colors:** Use subtle grays for borders, dividers, and secondary text.
- **Logo Usage:** Always display the Goldrock Financial logo in the header and footer. Maintain clear space around the logo and do not alter its colors.

## 2. Typography
- **Font Family:** Use a modern, clean sans-serif font (e.g., Montserrat, Open Sans, or similar).
- **Headings:** Use bold, uppercase headings for main sections (e.g., H1, H2).
- **Body Text:** Use regular weight, high-contrast text for readability.
- **Button/Text Links:** Use the gold color for primary actions and hover states.

## 3. Layout & Structure
- **Navigation:** Sticky top navigation bar with clear links (Home, About Us, Services, FAQ, Contact).
- **Hero Section:** Prominent hero section with a bold headline, subheadline, and a clear call-to-action button (e.g., "Apply Now").
- **Sections:** Use clear, distinct sections for services, process, testimonials, and contact.
- **Cards/Boxes:** Use card-style boxes with subtle shadows for service offerings (e.g., Term Loan, Line of Credit).
- **Footer:** Include contact info, quick links, and logo in the footer.

## 4. Imagery & Icons
- **Imagery:** Use high-quality, business-related images (e.g., professionals, offices, cityscapes).
- **Icons:** Use simple, line-style icons in gold or black for features and benefits.

## 5. Buttons & CTAs
- **Primary Button:** Gold background with black or white text, rounded corners, bold font.
- **Secondary Button:** Black or dark background with gold border/text.
- **Hover Effects:** Slight shadow or color darkening on hover.

## 6. Forms
- **Input Fields:** Rounded corners, subtle border, clear labels.
- **Button:** Gold with bold text.
- **Validation:** Clear error messages in red, success in green.

## 7. Responsiveness
- **Mobile First:** Ensure all layouts adapt gracefully to mobile devices.
- **Navigation:** Use a hamburger menu on mobile.
- **Touch Targets:** Make buttons and links large enough for easy tapping.

## 8. Accessibility
- **Contrast:** Ensure sufficient color contrast for all text and interactive elements.
- **Alt Text:** Provide descriptive alt text for all images and icons.
- **Keyboard Navigation:** All interactive elements must be accessible via keyboard.

## 9. Content & Tone
- **Voice:** Professional, confident, and approachable.
- **Headlines:** Action-oriented and benefit-focused (e.g., "Get your business loan deposited within 24 hours").
- **Trust Signals:** Highlight experience, speed, and customer service.

## 10. Consistency
- **Spacing:** Use consistent padding and margin throughout.
- **Component Reuse:** Use shared components for buttons, cards, and forms.
- **Style Guide:** Document all colors, fonts, and component styles for future reference.

# GoldRock Web Project Rules

## Project Structure
- All source code should be organized in the `src` directory
- Components should be placed in `src/components`
- Pages should be placed in `src/pages`
- Styles should be placed in `src/styles`
- Utilities and helpers should be placed in `src/utils`
- API-related code should be placed in `src/api`
- Types and interfaces should be placed in `src/types`

## Code Style
- Use TypeScript for all new code
- Follow ESLint and Prettier configurations
- Use functional components with hooks
- Implement proper type definitions for all props and state
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Add JSDoc comments for complex functions and components

## Component Guidelines
- Keep components small and focused on a single responsibility
- Use proper prop types and default props
- Implement error boundaries where necessary
- Follow atomic design principles
- Use CSS modules or styled-components for styling
- Ensure components are responsive and accessible

## State Management
- Use React Context for global state
- Implement proper state initialization
- Handle loading and error states
- Use proper state update patterns
- Implement proper data fetching patterns

## Testing
- Write unit tests for all components
- Implement integration tests for critical user flows
- Maintain good test coverage
- Use proper testing utilities and mocks
- Follow testing best practices

## Performance
- Implement proper code splitting
- Optimize images and assets
- Use proper caching strategies
- Implement lazy loading where appropriate
- Monitor and optimize bundle size

## Security
- Implement proper authentication and authorization
- Sanitize user inputs
- Use environment variables for sensitive data
- Follow security best practices
- Implement proper CORS policies

## Documentation
- Maintain up-to-date README
- Document complex business logic
- Add inline comments for non-obvious code
- Keep documentation in sync with code changes
- Use proper commit messages

## Git Workflow
- Use feature branches
- Follow conventional commits
- Create pull requests for all changes
- Require code reviews
- Keep commits atomic and focused

## Build and Deployment
- Use proper environment configurations
- Implement CI/CD pipelines
- Follow deployment best practices
- Monitor application performance
- Implement proper logging

## Accessibility
- Follow WCAG guidelines
- Implement proper ARIA attributes
- Ensure keyboard navigation
- Test with screen readers
- Maintain proper color contrast

## Browser Support
- Support modern browsers
- Implement proper polyfills
- Test across different browsers
- Handle browser-specific issues

- Maintain cross-browser compatibility 